<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · religion</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shaivism</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Saivism, Saiva</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shaivism is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> tradition that worships <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> as the supreme deity. Rooted in Vedic and later Puranic religion, it developed temple cults, ascetic orders and philosophical schools such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a> Shaivism and Shaiva Siddhanta. Many Indian and Southeast Asian dynasties were Shaiva patrons, marking their coins and inscriptions with Shiva's trident or his bull Nandi and building great temples to the linga.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shaivism.jpg" alt="Shaivism" width="320" height="460" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Worship_AS.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
